<article class="env-remark" id="0BNX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BNX">Remark <span data-tag="0BNX">15.92.11</span>.</a> Let $(R \to R', f)$ be a glueing pair and let $M$ be an $R$-module. Here are some observations which can be used to determine whether $M$ is glueable for $(R \to R', f)$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
By Lemma <a href="/tag/0BNW" data-tag="0BNW">15.92.10</a> we see that $M$ is glueable for $(R \to R^\wedge , f)$ if and only if $M[f^\infty ] \to M \otimes _ R R^\wedge $ is injective. This holds if $M[f] \to M^\wedge $ is injective, i.e., when $M[f] \cap \bigcap _{n = 1}^\infty f^ n M = 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\text{Tor}_1^ R(M, R'_ f) = 0$, then $M$ is glueable for $(R \to R', f)$ (use Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00M0" data-tag="00M0">10.75.2</a>). This is equivalent to saying that $\text{Tor}_1^ R(M, R')$ is $f$-power torsion. In particular, any flat $R$-module is glueable for $(R \to R', f)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $R \to R'$ is flat, then $\text{Tor}_1^ R(M, R') = 0$ for every $R$-module so every $R$-module is glueable for $(R \to R', f)$. This holds in particular when $R$ is Noetherian and $R' = R^\wedge $, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MB" data-tag="00MB">10.97.2</a> 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>